   a literal `'port1.0.1'` is simply wrong on chassis platforms (x8100, x908gen2,
   x908gen3) and on an x950 whose card slot is populated — those use `port1.1.x`. It is a
   runtime property of the hardware, not something you can infer from the case text.
4c. **ASSERT ON THE FEATURE UNDER TEST, not merely that the link survived.** If the step
   enables/disables a feature, the `passed()`/`failed()` condition must read that
   feature's own reported state. Checking only `Link is UP` after disabling a feature
   passes whenever the disable SILENTLY FAILS — a false green, and the single most common
   defect found in review. Use the feature's own show command from the reference above
   (EcoMode/LPI/EEE → `show ecofriendly`, whose per-port `Configured`/`Status` columns
   prove it). `Configured` = the config you applied, `Status` = what the port is doing
   now: after the `no` form, `Configured` must read `off` — asserting the feature is
   still `Configured` after disabling contradicts the step. `Status` may lag
   `Configured` (peer hasn't negotiated), so use `Configured` for "did the command take"
   and `Status` only for operational state. Link stability may be an EXTRA assertion, never the
   only one. Enter interface config mode before applying an interface-level command —
   a global-mode `ecofriendly lpi` configures nothing.
4d. **PARSE THE ROW FOR YOUR PORT — a bare substring test is a false green.** Per-port
   tables (`show ecofriendly`, `show interface status`) print one row per port, so
   `if 'off' in output:` is True whenever ANY port reads `off` — including when the
   command under test did nothing. Select the line for the port first, then test that
   line's field:
   ```python
   row = next((l for l in output.splitlines() if l.split()[:1] == [port]), None)
   if row is None:
       self.failed('port {} missing from show ecofriendly output'.format(port))
   elif row.split()[-2] == 'off':             # Configured column ONLY
       self.passed(...)
   ```
   **Assert ONE named column, never a tuple of adjacent columns.** `show ecofriendly` rows
   are `<port> [<name>] <Configured> <Status>`, so `Configured` is `split()[-2]` and
   `Status` is `split()[-1]`. Testing BOTH positionally (`split()[-2:] == ['off','off']`)
   is wrong and fails on real hardware: `Status` legitimately reads `-` on a port with no
   peer (`port1.0.7  off  -`) and lags `Configured` while negotiating
   (`port1.0.5  lpi  off`). Both are normal states, so a two-column check is a false RED
   after a command that actually worked. Pick the column the step is about and test that
   one.
   Index from the END, not a fixed column: `show ecofriendly`'s optional `Name` column
   shifts the middle fields (`port1.0.1 Port 1 lpi lpi` splits to 5 tokens,
   `port1.0.2 lpi lpi` to 3), but `Configured`/`Status` are always the last two.
   Match the port name EXACTLY — `line.startswith(port)` also matches `port1.0.10` when
   `port` is `port1.0.1`, silently reading the wrong row. Compare the first token
   (`l.split()[:1] == [port]`) as shown above.
   Same rule for any `show` whose output covers more than the port under test.
